{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Computer Security Specialist"}, {"type": "position", "content": 2}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "A2.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to describe computer security specialist job."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Use present tense to talk about duties and occupations in negative, e.g., They do not have to take care of networks, they work hand to hand with network engineers.  Employ some idioms on technology with a specific meaning in conversations with colleagues."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Understand important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level.  Exchange simple information on the design of an information system, computer architecture and hardware, and basic concepts on graphic design."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n4le01ob02re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Simple Present Tense Review to Talk About Duties and Occupations in Negative Sentences"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use the <span class=\"strong\">simple present tense</span>  to talk about daily habits, facts, and duties."}, {"type": "example", "content": "They don't have to take care of networks all the time. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "We don't have to manage the company's security alone because we can count on a professional for that matter."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Negative form</span> <br/><br/>  You use the following structure to make negative statements in <span class=\"strong\">simple present</span>:  <div class=\"center_text\">Subject + Auxiliary verb <span class=\"strong\">Do/ Does + Not</span> + Main verb in base form + Complement</div><br/><br/>  Examples:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">They <span class=\"strong\">do not have</span> to take care of networks.<br/> She <span class=\"strong\">doesn't work</span> as an IT specialist</span>.<br/><br/>  Notice that once you use the auxiliary verb does with the third person singular (<span class=\"strong\">he, she</span>, and <span class=\"strong\">it</span>), the main verb drops the <span class=\"strong\">s</span> or <span class=\"strong\">\u2013es</span> used in affirmative statements.<br/><br/>  Examples: <br/> <span class=\"italic\">She work<span class=\"strong\">s</span> at a software company. (affirmative form)<br/> She <span class=\"strong\">doesn't work</span> at a software company. (negative form)</span><br/><br/>  These are the two auxiliaries you use in the negative form of the <span class=\"strong\">present simple tense</span>:<br/><br/>"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":2,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div class=\\\"center_text\\\"> <span class=\\\"strong\\\"> DO </span></div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div class=\\\"center_text\\\"> <span class=\\\"strong\\\"> DOES </span></div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"I<br />  You<br />  We<br />  They<br />\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"He<br /> She<br /> It<br />\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\"\"}"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Idioms on Technology"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Idioms </span> are important in all languages since they become a different way of saying something with a new connotation."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I don't think we need that many computer specialists; it can be like reinventing the wheel! "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Because you are the expert; it's not rocket science!"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Some of these idioms come from the technology field. As examples, here you have these three common idioms:"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":5,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div class=\\\"center_text\\\"> <span class=\\\"strong\\\"> TECHNOLOGY IDIOMS</span></div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div class=\\\"center_text\\\"> <span class=\\\"strong\\\">Idiom</span></div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"It is not difficult to do it\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\">1. It's not rocket science.</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"To finish doing something\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\">2. Pull the plug</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"To finish doing something.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\">3. Reinvent the wheel</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"To do something that is already done; to try to come up with a new way of doing something when there is already a way to do it\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "image", "content": "enpe8n4le01ob02img01.jpg"}, {"type": "image", "content": "enpe8n4le01ob02img02.jpg"}, {"type": "image", "content": "enpe8n4le01ob02img03.jpg"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "threaten"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to menace"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example:  Imagine that we have a security problem that threatens to make us lose our information systems. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n4le01ob02aud01"